                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "fb74d144-aacd-3ae5-810a-ae282666c7c0",
                        "entry_id": "ddd79a2b-ec86-3aad-9ed6-cedee7fc21c3",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Donner un caractère racial à."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3a6c0263-338e-4865-8992-d4b03100005a",
                                "sense_id": "fb74d144-aacd-3ae5-810a-ae282666c7c0",
                                "text": "Les uns et les autres sont souvent parasités par les préjugés de leurs extrêmes, qui semblent s’être donné le mot tantôt pour « indigéniser » tantôt pour « raciser » la question sociale."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "05949f29-f3a4-465f-8dd1-ca05387a76e8",
                                "sense_id": "fb74d144-aacd-3ae5-810a-ae282666c7c0",
                                "text": "Raciser une population, c’est l’enfermer dans un passé sans présent ni avenir : l’assigner à être une Race sans histoire."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "68061105-0eb8-4936-98ba-3347cf1322a7",
                                "sense_id": "fb74d144-aacd-3ae5-810a-ae282666c7c0",
                                "text": "Pour Khan, le mot “racisé” est “un mot qui nous sépare” : “Cette assignation-là, je la récuse,” dit-elle. Pour Diallo, “racisé fait état d’un processus, et donc c’est un statut sociologique qui dit que des personnes, du fait d’un contexte donné, sont confrontées à des expériences de vie qui les racisent.” Selon elle, “tout le monde est racisé : les Blancs sont aussi racisés, à leur avantage.” Pour Daniel Schneidermann, c’est le signe qu’il faut abandonner le mot, ce à quoi Rokhaya Diallo répond qu’elle l’emploie “très peu” et lui préfère “personnes non-blanches”."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "7eb856f2-2139-3a94-9064-3197898944e8",
                        "entry_id": "ddd79a2b-ec86-3aad-9ed6-cedee7fc21c3",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Prendre une dimension raciale."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "pronominal"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "c3b9ffc5-4367-44d1-8ee6-ae22e6c0b25c",
                        "source_entry_id": "ddd79a2b-ec86-3aad-9ed6-cedee7fc21c3",
                        "target_lang_word": "racialize",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "racialize",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8231fabf-bf4b-3b57-bb16-7d8c60495685",
                                        "entry_id": "fdb58a4a-f25d-37d6-8307-607ac515262c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To categorize or treat in racial terms."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c85ec76b-1a7e-4b87-a210-94ab365b62d8",
                                                "sense_id": "8231fabf-bf4b-3b57-bb16-7d8c60495685",
                                                "text": "There’s a growing consensus that a large proportion of racialized decisions is not driven by any conscious race discrimination, but that it is often just driven by unconscious, or subconscious, attitudes."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a68ce2c9-97f1-3bab-ba2c-e3036fc96d03",
                                        "entry_id": "fdb58a4a-f25d-37d6-8307-607ac515262c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To adapt or alter to conform with the ethnic qualities of a particular race."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "306d50bb-bdcb-4e92-a096-af7b36a38052",
                                                "sense_id": "a68ce2c9-97f1-3bab-ba2c-e3036fc96d03",
                                                "text": "If Mr. [W. B.] Yeats had not broken clean away from his Irish forerunners his poetry would not have delighted anybody save the resolute patriot. We say this not because we wish to rob Ireland of her legitimate glory, but because we think her glory will be dimmed if Irish poetry be too selfishly racialized."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "aa891bd3-ede7-408b-bc2c-94a7a221c5ef",
                                                "sense_id": "a68ce2c9-97f1-3bab-ba2c-e3036fc96d03",
                                                "text": "As a race, negroes sing well and dance well, but they have made no distinct contribution to the play-life of nations. It is a matter of no little significance that the negro, while he has been singing his own melodies into the heats of all nations, has yet no folk-games, no race, pageants, no adequate rhythmic movements to accompany his own music. His games are pitiful imitations. The dance, to the degree he has racialized it, is not among the achievements he is proud to claim."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
